<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.13.4: Solving Inequality Problems Using Tables</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00611</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.13.4: Solving Inequality Problems Using Tables</md:title>
    <md:uuid>c58a752d-7e07-47dd-bac5-7a99239ca145</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00001">      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">
        
          <media alt="A close-up of a colorful trail mix containing dried apricots, raisins, white chocolate chips, walnuts, hazelnuts, dried cranberries, and other mixed nuts and dried fruits.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_09_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
     </para>
      <para id="para-00002">For questions 1 – 5, use the following scenario to graph an inequality from a table:</para>
      <para id="para-00003">
        <quote id="quote-00001">Andre is making trail mix with dried fruit and mixed nuts. Dried fruit costs $3 per
        pound and mixed nuts cost $6 per pound. He wants to spend no more than $54 on the trail mix.
        The inequality <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>18</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> represents the number of pounds of dried fruit, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, he
        can buy for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> pounds of nuts.</quote>
     </para>
      <!--Q#1-->

      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">The related equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>18</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> represents the boundary line of the
            inequality. Complete the table of values to find points on the boundary line of the
            inequality.</para>
          <table class="unnumbered os-raise-skinnytable unnumbered" id="table-00001">
            <tgroup cols="2">
              <thead>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></emphasis>
                  </entry>
                </row>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                <row>
                  <entry>0</entry>
                  <entry/>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>2</entry>
                  <entry/>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>4</entry>
                  <entry/>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>6</entry>
                  <entry/>
                </row>
              </tbody>
            </tgroup>
          </table>

          <para id="para-00005">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
            compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00006">Compare your answer:</para>
          <table class="unnumbered os-raise-skinnytable unnumbered" id="table-00002">
            <tgroup cols="2">
              <thead>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></emphasis>
                  </entry>
                </row>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                <row>
                  <entry>0</entry>
                  <entry>18</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>2</entry>
                  <entry>14</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>4</entry>
                  <entry>10</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>6</entry>
                  <entry>6</entry>
                </row>
              </tbody>
            </tgroup>
          </table>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#2-->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <!--QUESTION
          IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS-->
          <para id="para-00007">Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph the data from your table
            of values that represents this scenario using the Desmos tool below.</para>
        <media alt="insert alt text">
        <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/>
        </media>
          <para id="para-00008">When you have finished graphing, select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
            compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00009">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00010">
                  <media alt="Scatter plot titled Trail Mix showing a negative correlation between mixed nuts and dried fruit. The weight of mixed nuts in pounds is represented on the x-axis. The weight of the dried fruit in pounds is represented on the y-axis.">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_09_02.jpg" width="300px"/>
              </media>
            
         </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#3-->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00011">Is the boundary line for the inequality <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>18</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> a solid line or a dashed
            line? Explain how you know.</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00012">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00013">The line is solid because the inequality <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>≤</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> means the points on the line are
            included as solutions.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#4-->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00014">Is the point <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> a solution to the inequality?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00015">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00016">Yes, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>18</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mn>18</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#5-->

      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00017">Use the graph from number 2. Enter the inequality <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>18</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> to represent
            the data. Notice the shading for the solution region of this inequality.</para>
          <para id="para-00018">When you have finished graphing, select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
            compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00019">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00020">
            
              <media alt="A linear inequality graph titled Trail Mix shows pounds of dried fruit on the y-axis and the pounds of mixed nuts on the x-axis. The boundary line slopes down from 18 pounds dried fruit and 0 mixed nuts, to 0 pounds dried fruit and 9 pounds mixed nuts.">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_09_03.jpg" width="300px"/>
              </media>
            
         </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <para id="para-00021">In problems 6–10, use the data from the table to graph the inequality and write an
        inequality with two variables.</para>
      <table class="unnumbered os-raise-skinnytable unnumbered" id="table-00003">
        <tgroup cols="2">
          <thead>
            <row>
              <entry>
                <emphasis><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></emphasis>
              </entry>
              <entry>
                <emphasis><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></emphasis>
              </entry>
            </row>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <entry>-2</entry>
              <entry>-5</entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>0</entry>
              <entry>-6</entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>2</entry>
              <entry>1</entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>3</entry>
              <entry>5</entry>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </tgroup>
      </table>
      
 
        <!--Q#6-->

      <exercise id="exer-00006">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <!--QUESTION
          IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS-->
          <para id="para-00022">Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph the data from your table
            of values that represents this scenario using the Desmos tool below.</para>
        <media alt="insert alt text">
        <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/>
        </media>
          <para id="para-00023">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
            compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00024">Compare your answer:</para>
          
            <media alt="A graph with four labeled points: (3, 5), (2, 1), (negative 2, negative 5) and (0, negative 6).">
              <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_09_04.png"/>
            </media>
          
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#7-->

      <exercise id="exer-00007">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00025">Now, in the Desmos window graph <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00026">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
            compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00027">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00028">
            
              <media alt="A graph with a line passing through four labeled points: (3, 5), (2, 1), (negative 2, negative 5) and (0, negative 6).">
                <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_09_05.png"/>
              </media>
            
         </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#8-->
      <exercise id="exer-00008">
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00029">How does the line need to move to capture all of the points?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00008">
          <para id="para-00030">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00031">Answers may vary.</para>
          <para id="para-00032">Increase the <emphasis effect="italics">y</emphasis>-intercept or the slope to
            capture the point <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#9-->
      <exercise id="exer-00009">
        <problem id="prob-00009">
          <para id="para-00033">Using desmos, adjust the slope and/or the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept to find an inequality that
            could be a possibility.</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00009">
          <para id="para-00034">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00035">Answers may vary.</para>
          <para id="para-00036">These are possible representation of where the line could on the graph</para>
     
          <para id="para-00037"><emphasis>Less than</emphasis> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para>
              <media alt="Graph of an inequality on the coordinate plane. The region is shaded red below and to the right of the dashed boundary line. Four points lie in the solution region.">
                <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_09_06.png"/>
              </media>
            
          <para id="para-00038"><emphasis>Less than or equal to</emphasis> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para> 
            
              <media alt="Graph of an inequality on the coordinate plane. The region is shaded red below and to the right of the solid boundary line. Four points lie in the solution region.">
                <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_09_07.png"/>
              </media>
            
          <para id="para-00039"><emphasis>Greater than</emphasis> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            
              <media alt="Graph of an inequality on the coordinate plane. The region is shaded red above and to the left of the dashed boundary line. Four points lie in the solution region.">
                <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_09_08.png"/>
              </media>
            
          <para id="para-00040"><emphasis>Greater than or equal to</emphasis> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          
              <media alt="Graph of an inequality on the coordinate plane. The region is shaded red above and to the left of the solid boundary line. Four points lie in the solution region.">
                <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_09_09.png"/>
              </media>
            
          <para id="para-00041">
            <emphasis>Not a solution</emphasis></para>
              
              <media alt="Graph of an inequality on the coordinate plane. The region is shaded red below and to the right of the dashed boundary line. Only three points lie in the solution region. One point does not satisfy the inequality.">
                <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_09_10.png"/>
              </media>
            
          </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#10-->

      <exercise id="exer-00010">
        <problem id="prob-00010">
          <para id="para-00042">Use the graph from number 7. Shade the correct side of the boundary line to
            represent the inequality <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00043">When you have finished plotting the points, select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis>
            button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00010">
          <para id="para-00044">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00045">
            
              <media alt="Graph of an inequalilty on the coordinate plane.">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_09_11.jpg" width="300px"/>
              </media>
            
         </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
    </section>

    <section id="sect-00002">
      <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
        <para id="para-00046">Which table of values can be used to graph the boundary line
        of the inequality <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>?</para>
      </note>
    </section>

    <section id="sect-00003">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00004">
        <title>Connecting Representations of Inequalities</title>
        <para id="para-00047">Tables can be used to help you to write and solve inequalities in two variables.</para>
        <para id="para-00048"><emphasis>Part 1:</emphasis> Writing Inequalities in Two Variables Given A Set of Data
          in a Table</para>
        <para id="para-00049">Write a linear inequality in two variables based on the given data:</para>
        <table class="unnumbered os-raise-skinnytable unnumbered" id="table-00004">
          <tgroup cols="2">
            <thead>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></emphasis>
                </entry>
              </row>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
              <row>
                <entry>-4</entry>
                <entry>-3</entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>-2</entry>
                <entry>-2</entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>0</entry>
                <entry>-1</entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>2</entry>
                <entry>0</entry>
              </row>
            </tbody>
          </tgroup>
        </table>

        <para id="para-00050">Remember that for an inequality the points could be on the line, above the the line,
          or below the line. The type of inequality will differ based on these conditions.</para>
        <table class="unnumbered os-raise-horizontaltable unnumbered" id="table-00005">
          <tgroup cols="2">
            <tbody>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis>On the line</emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>≤</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>≥</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis>Above the line</emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry><m:math><m:mrow><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis>Below the line</emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry><m:math><m:mrow><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></entry>
              </row>
            </tbody>
          </tgroup>
        </table>

        <para id="para-00051">If you are given a set of possible points like the table above, there could be
          multiple lines that the data points could represent. Let’s figure out a few possibilities
          for this given set.</para>
        <para id="para-00052"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Graph the points on a coordinate plane.</para>
  
          
            <media alt="A coordinate plane with five labeled points: (5, 8), (3, 5), (1, 1), (3, -5), and (-4, -6). Each point is marked with a green dot and labeled with its coordinates.">
              <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_11_01.png"/>
            </media>
          

        <para id="para-00053"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Determine possible lines.</para>
        <para id="para-00054">Remember that the dots can be on the line, but they all have to be either above or
          below to create an inequality. If the line has points on both sides, then the line isn’t a
          possibility.</para>
        <para id="para-00055">Let’s try a few possibilities. We can start with the equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> to see if the
          points are above the line or below the line.</para>
 
          
            <media alt="A graph of a red line that has a positive slope passes through points (0,0) and (1, 1). Additional green points are at (3,5), (5, 8), (negative 4, negative 6) and (3, negative 5). All points are labeled with their coordinates.">
              <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_11_02.png"/>
            </media>
          

        <para id="para-00056">The equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> cannot be a possibility because there are points above and below
          the line.</para>
        <para id="para-00057"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Adjust the slope or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept</para>
        <para id="para-00058">We can change the slope and the <emphasis effect="italics">y</emphasis>-intercept to
          see how we can shift the line to be able to capture all the points. Let’s move the line to
          below the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. We can leave the slope as 1 and change the <emphasis effect="italics">y</emphasis>-intercept to -7.</para>

          
            <media alt="A graph of a red line with positive slope passing through it. Labeled points are at (3, 5), (5, 8), (1, 1), (-4, -6), and (3, -5) marked with green dots. the line does not intersect any of the labeled points.">
              <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_11_03.png" width="300px"/>
            </media>
          

        <para id="para-00059">There is still one point below the line. We could decrease the slope of the line to
          <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>13</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>

          
            <media alt="A graph showing five green points labeled (3, 5), (5, 8), (1, 1), (3, -5), and (-4, -6), with a red line that has a positive slope. The line does not intersect any of the labeled points.">
              <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_11_04.png" width="300px"/>
            </media>
          

        <para id="para-00060">Now all of the points are above the line. This means that the value of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is always
          greater than the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> value.</para>
        <para id="para-00061">So, one of the possible solutions could be <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mn>13</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        
        <para id="para-00062"><emphasis>Part 2: Using Tables to Solve Inequalities in Two Variables</emphasis></para>
        <para id="para-00063">Graph the linear inequality <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> using a table.</para>
        <para id="para-00064"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Create a table of values using the related equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> to identify points on the boundary line of the inequality.</para>
        
          <table class="unnumbered os-raise-skinnytable unnumbered" id="table-00006">
          <tgroup cols="2">
            <thead>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></emphasis>
                </entry>
              </row>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
              <row>
                <entry>−4</entry>
                <entry>−3</entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>−2</entry>
                <entry>−2</entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>0</entry>
                <entry>-1</entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>2</entry>
                <entry>0</entry>
              </row>
            </tbody>
          </tgroup>
        </table>

        <para id="para-00065"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Plot the points and sketch the boundary line.</para>
        <para id="para-00066">If the inequality is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>≤</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>≥</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>, the boundary line is solid.</para>
        <para id="para-00067">If the inequality is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>, the boundary line is dashed.</para>
        <para id="para-00068">The inequality sign is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>, so we draw a dashed line.</para>
 
          
            <media alt="A graph with a dashed teal line passing through points (-4, -3), (-2, -2), (0, -1), (2, 0), and (4, 1) on a grid. The line has a positive slope and crosses the y-axis at -1.">
              <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_11_05.jpg" width="300px"/>
            </media>
          

        <para id="para-00069"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Test a point that is not on the boundary line. Is it a
          solution of the inequality?</para>
        <para id="para-00070">Test <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> as it is not on the boundary line of the inequality.</para>
        <para id="para-00071"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mover><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mo>?</m:mo></m:mover><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00072"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00073"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> is False.</para>
        <para id="para-00074">So, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> is not a solution.</para>
        
        <para id="para-00075"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Shade in one side of the boundary line.</para>
        <para id="para-00076">If the test point is a solution, shade in the side that includes the point.</para>
        <para id="para-00077">If the test point is not a solution, shade in the opposite side.</para>
        <para id="para-00078">The test point <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> is not a solution, so shade in the side of the boundary line
          that does not include <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>

          
            <media alt="A graph with a shaded region below the dashed line representing y equals one-half times x minus 1.">
              <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_11_06.jpg" width="300px"/>
            </media>
          

        <para id="para-00079">All points in the shaded region and not on the boundary line represent the solutions
          to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>

        <note class="try look-at-me" id="note-00002">
        <title>Try It: Graph Linear Inequalities in Two Variables</title>

        <exercise id="exer-00011">
          <problem id="prob-00011">
            <para id="para-00080">Is the inequality  <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> a solution for the table of values? Explain your
              reasoning.</para>
            <table class="unnumbered os-raise-skinnytable unnumbered" id="table-00007">
              <tgroup cols="2">
                <thead>
                  <row>
                    <entry>
                      <emphasis><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></emphasis>
                    </entry>
                    <entry>
                      <emphasis><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></emphasis>
                    </entry>
                  </row>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                  <row>
                    <entry>-4</entry>
                    <entry>-3</entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry>-2</entry>
                    <entry>-2</entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry>0</entry>
                    <entry>-1</entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry>2</entry>
                    <entry>0</entry>
                  </row>
                </tbody>
              </tgroup>
            </table>

            <para id="para-00081">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
              compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00011">
            <para id="para-00082">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00083">No, the point <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> sits on the line so it can’t be greater than or less
              than.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

        <!--Interaction
        End -->
        <!--Q#-->
        <!--Interaction
        End -->

        <exercise id="exer-00012">
          <problem id="prob-00012">
            <para id="para-00084">Use a table to graph the inequality <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
            <para id="para-00085">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
              compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00012">
            <para id="para-00086">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00087">Here’s how to solve an inequality using a table:</para>
            <para id="para-00088"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Create a table of values using the related equation
              <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> to identify points on the boundary line of the inequality.</para>
            <table class="unnumbered os-raise-skinnytable unnumbered" id="table-00008">
              <tgroup cols="2">
                <thead>
                  <row>
                    <entry>
                      <emphasis>x</emphasis>
                    </entry>
                    <entry>
                      <emphasis>y</emphasis>
                    </entry>
                  </row>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                  <row>
                    <entry>−4</entry>
                    <entry>−5</entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry>−2</entry>
                    <entry>−1</entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry>0</entry>
                    <entry>3</entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry>2</entry>
                    <entry>7</entry>
                  </row>
                </tbody>
              </tgroup>
            </table>

            <para id="para-00089"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Plot the points and sketch the boundary line.</para>
            <para id="para-00090">If the inequality is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>≤</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>≥</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>, the boundary line is solid.</para>
            <para id="para-00091">If the inequality is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>, the boundary line is dashed.</para>
            <para id="para-00092">The inequality sign is , so we draw a solid line.</para>
    
              
                <media alt="A graph with a dashed teal line passing through points (-4, -5), (-2, -1), (0, 3), and (2, 7) on a grid. The line has a positive slope and crosses the y-axis at 3.">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_11_07.jpg" width="300px"/>
                </media>
              
  
            <para id="para-00093"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Test a point that is not on the boundary line. Is it
              a solution of the inequality?</para>
            <para id="para-00094">Test <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> as it is not on the boundary line of the inequality.</para>
            <para id="para-00095"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00096"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>\stackrel</m:mi><m:mrow><m:mo>?</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mo>≤</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00097"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> is True.</para>
            <para id="para-00098">So, (0, 0) is a solution.</para>
            <para id="para-00099"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Shade in one side of the boundary line.</para>
            <para id="para-00100">If the test point is a solution, shade in the side that includes the point.</para>
            <para id="para-00101">If the test point is not a solution, shade in the opposite side.</para>
            <para id="para-00102">The test point <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> is a solution, so shade in the side of the boundary line
              that includes <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      
              
                <media alt="A graph with a shaded region below the dashed line representing y equals two times x plus 3.">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_11_08.jpg" width="300px"/>
                </media>
              
      
            <para id="para-00103">All points in the shaded region and not on the boundary line represent the
              solutions to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
</note>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>
